  'Ethnographic Area Studies',
  'Students ask what a region is, who defines it, and how ethnography can study place without freezing cultures into bounded containers. They build historical depth for contemporary cases, design responsible regionally informed research, and analyze colonialism, language and identity, kinship and personhood, symbolic life, political economy, mobility, land and climate, and governance without reducing culture to policy. The course adds comparative and multi-sited analysis, ethics and representation, and a regional research capstone.',
  $ethajson${"slug": "ethnographic-area-studies", "title": "Ethnographic Area Studies", "description": "Students ask what a region is, who defines it, and how ethnography can study place without freezing cultures into bounded containers. They build historical depth for contemporary cases, design responsible regionally informed research, and analyze colonialism, language and identity, kinship and personhood, symbolic life, political economy, mobility, land and climate, and governance without reducing culture to policy. The course adds comparative and multi-sited analysis, ethics and representation, and a regional research capstone.", "topics": [{"id": "6751200e-7f2f-46ea-b476-570277970ca6", "type": "topic", "title": "Area Studies, Anthropology, and the Problem of Region", "description": "Students begin by asking what a region is, who defines it, and how ethnography can study place without freezing cultures into bounded containers.", "children": [{"id": "02bc25ed-d4d0-42ce-890c-569ff2b54209", "type": "subtopic", "title": "What counts as a region", "children": [{"id": "b42d3c46-1c46-42e0-af9f-e4d10ed6ef5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Culture areas, nation-states, borderlands, oceans, diasporas, and transregional circuits"}, {"id": "5e528ef7-5ea7-41c2-aadb-042f4b142b8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Scale: village, city, province, nation, region, empire, and globe"}, {"id": "0edf9f5e-64b4-4058-859e-0d22e555b450", "type": "concept", "title": "Risks of essentialism, exceptionalism, and area stereotypes"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces region as an analytic, historical, political, and lived category rather than a neutral map label."}, {"id": "e3c0fcdc-b632-4da3-9d9c-69b15bb2845c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Anthropology and area studies", "children": [{"id": "ccad6cfc-84f1-40ad-9fcb-39b04e040ed9", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnography, participant observation, and local categories of meaning"}, {"id": "943d368f-7bc2-4b72-9d41-c110eefae4f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparison with political science, history, sociology, geography, and international relations"}, {"id": "af6872e2-34b5-4769-a511-8bb515eb3bec", "type": "concept", "title": "Area expertise, language study, archives, media, policy, and field access"}], "description": "Students learn how anthropology contributes grounded, everyday, language-sensitive knowledge to broader regional scholarship."}, {"id": "a9084f21-79c2-49f9-819e-92ea67acb1bd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reflexivity and positionality", "children": [{"id": "5347ba9b-fccf-44d8-8f08-a20befa5589a", "type": "concept", "title": "Researcher identity, insider/outsider status, and accountability"}, {"id": "e41b9b54-bb47-4641-b1d3-8610522fd5c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Orientalism, colonial knowledge, extraction, and representation"}, {"id": "4049724c-7cdc-47f4-9a6b-a31a2cc5eaec", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethical humility, uncertainty, and responsible claims about place"}], "description": "This subtopic frames area research as a relationship shaped by biography, power, language, mobility, and institutional location."}]}, {"id": "30f94f8c-579b-420e-b6b6-407133eadfd9", "type": "topic", "title": "Regional Histories and Anthropological Debates", "description": "Students build enough historical depth to understand contemporary ethnographic cases as products of long-term social, political, and cultural change.", "children": [{"id": "20c0df8c-44ee-48ed-b5b0-dfe8cdd860ab", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical foundations", "children": [{"id": "b73bd130-e28c-4536-ba5f-1a7d969ac766", "type": "concept", "title": "Precolonial formations, state systems, trade networks, and religious movements"}, {"id": "a95caf4f-148a-4e87-ad08-027fd63b3691", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial rule, border making, labor regimes, and missionization"}, {"id": "dad0d38c-f5fa-4714-aecf-1044800424c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Independence, revolution, development projects, and contemporary transformations"}], "description": "Area studies requires students to see the present as historically produced, not as timeless culture."}, {"id": "ee0e0e37-390b-416b-8808-868e67a3eefd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Key anthropological debates by region", "children": [{"id": "34a69251-a95f-41eb-9a4c-69d31e4b523b", "type": "concept", "title": "Classic ethnographies, revisionist scholarship, and contemporary field debates"}, {"id": "70e80124-ab34-4033-919c-67bf3e0f4edf", "type": "concept", "title": "Local concepts of personhood, authority, morality, economy, and belonging"}, {"id": "fc8c7ebf-412a-4a31-8aee-f22b3c8947b2", "type": "concept", "title": "Debates over tradition, modernity, nationalism, globalization, and indigeneity"}], "description": "Students learn to identify the arguments that have shaped scholarship in the region they choose."}, {"id": "1b5837e0-3f98-4fa4-9643-646e8e5a8c4e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading across disciplines", "children": [{"id": "1a946926-77a9-467b-bec9-6581290bbb37", "type": "concept", "title": "Scholarly monographs, articles, archives, oral histories, and primary sources"}, {"id": "cde11604-4afd-449a-ae74-d628410015a9", "type": "concept", "title": "News, government documents, NGO reports, cultural production, and social media"}, {"id": "1dc8840a-e9f0-40aa-bc7b-8f5023686ed6", "type": "concept", "title": "Evaluating disciplinary assumptions and evidentiary standards"}], "description": "This subtopic teaches students to combine ethnography with history, political economy, media, and policy sources."}]}, {"id": "5f8cb2bb-d95e-40d8-a21f-3ba8f9af9af5", "type": "topic", "title": "Ethnographic Methods for Area Research", "description": "Students learn how to design responsible, regionally informed research using observation, interviews, fieldnotes, archives, and comparative evidence.", "children": [{"id": "995c0412-26e7-407d-89a9-4a9b7087a40a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fieldsite selection and research design", "children": [{"id": "20a9d5ba-6a90-4ccf-a976-7c2b1b8ea98b", "type": "concept", "title": "Research questions, case selection, access, feasibility, and language needs"}, {"id": "5cba0032-49a1-412b-930e-3aa701dcab7e", "type": "concept", "title": "Single-sited, multi-sited, virtual, institutional, and archival fieldwork"}, {"id": "41470754-f131-4cc9-a598-922f3e68a195", "type": "concept", "title": "Sampling, interlocutor diversity, triangulation, and scope control"}], "description": "This subtopic turns regional curiosity into a workable research question and evidence plan."}, {"id": "758cf3de-003e-410e-9cdc-04751f011dd6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Participant observation and interviewing", "children": [{"id": "43f14567-4516-4ef8-9789-8554c2cadc20", "type": "concept", "title": "Observation, participation, rapport, field roles, and everyday routines"}, {"id": "16a1b838-9b38-4227-9986-24c689844d5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Semi-structured interviews, oral histories, life histories, and focus groups"}, {"id": "eea60f59-3161-46e2-be60-a8c4705c71ca", "type": "concept", "title": "Recording, transcription, translation, confidentiality, and follow-up"}], "description": "Students practice the core methods that make ethnographic area studies attentive to lived experience."}, {"id": "c74e4abe-62cd-492f-9dae-d73723026dec", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fieldnotes, coding, and analysis", "children": [{"id": "52629c54-516a-41cf-a6b7-accf9f55effe", "type": "concept", "title": "Jottings, expanded fieldnotes, analytic memos, and reflexive notes"}, {"id": "5e2642da-9a44-42b2-96be-a2367ab7eb1d", "type": "concept", "title": "Coding, thematic analysis, narrative analysis, and negative cases"}, {"id": "6f581035-2d08-43fc-a959-39ceaf288f4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Building claims from evidence while preserving ambiguity and context"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how raw experience becomes organized evidence through disciplined writing and interpretation."}]}, {"id": "41c9782d-2554-4379-9ddf-1ed272a0c36d", "type": "topic", "title": "Colonialism, Postcoloniality, and Global Power", "description": "Students analyze how colonial rule, empire, capitalism, and global hierarchy continue to shape regional identities, institutions, and inequalities.", "children": [{"id": "ec6a0186-2d18-47ed-9cc3-1575c2fb0f2f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Colonial knowledge and governance", "children": [{"id": "dd2bda25-dce1-4354-ab52-e04bb0ed2095", "type": "concept", "title": "Census, maps, law, ethnology, museums, missions, schools, and archives"}, {"id": "3c719fa8-4512-42e0-929a-9699891dd381", "type": "concept", "title": "Indirect rule, settler colonialism, plantations, extraction, and labor migration"}, {"id": "ea0499b4-6f32-4cda-b3e5-f33543086886", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, tribe, caste, ethnicity, religion, and administrative categories"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how colonial institutions categorized people, land, labor, religion, and culture."}, {"id": "de609975-326b-4773-9988-a9ed959429e4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postcolonial states and memory", "children": [{"id": "c2ca6609-7bbb-4d11-88c9-24ebff5a5ffc", "type": "concept", "title": "Nation building, citizenship, borders, minorities, and official histories"}, {"id": "e9308f13-a2fc-45f4-9e37-9bbde33bfe27", "type": "concept", "title": "Resistance, decolonization, liberation movements, and civil conflict"}, {"id": "80a6aaea-2846-49d5-949e-5f935f1c8106", "type": "concept", "title": "Heritage, monuments, museums, reparations, and contested memory"}], "description": "Students study how independence, nationalism, violence, and memory politics shape present-day social life."}, {"id": "b9f4f81a-cfe5-45db-a5c6-619ddcfe2192", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global capitalism and unequal connection", "children": [{"id": "359a91be-28cc-4bdc-a02b-b4d21d3ca2e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Dependency, world systems, structural adjustment, debt, and development expertise"}, {"id": "e5b6a46b-27a4-4ec4-8274-a1639a9fda67", "type": "concept", "title": "Commodity frontiers, logistics, tourism, migration, and remittances"}, {"id": "d71773d6-4aea-4cb8-9812-4928a173e229", "type": "concept", "title": "NGOs, international organizations, corporations, and humanitarian power"}], "description": "This subtopic connects local lives to commodity chains, finance, aid, development, and geopolitics."}]}, {"id": "9890ea01-de9d-4b00-b3a0-10f91687cc9f", "type": "topic", "title": "Language, Translation, Ethnicity, and Identity", "description": "Students learn how language, naming, translation, and identity categories organize social life and shape what researchers can understand.", "children": [{"id": "3f46d545-6669-4bf5-8e22-d909aa88ab06", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language and cultural fluency", "children": [{"id": "a7445d4d-b959-4cf5-be43-b0272ee96686", "type": "concept", "title": "Language learning plans, interpreters, translation, transcription, and glossing"}, {"id": "85579318-85a2-4f3b-9d7f-cb8befc4ae82", "type": "concept", "title": "Code-switching, multilingualism, honorifics, speech genres, and pragmatics"}, {"id": "1c7e8313-8bdb-498d-a917-8ad666f0f3e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Untranslatable concepts, emic terms, and analytic translation choices"}], "description": "Area expertise depends on more than vocabulary; it requires attention to registers, genres, humor, silence, and local categories."}, {"id": "4f48a348-d5b6-46c3-8dce-d63dcf3d4b62", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethnicity, race, caste, indigeneity, and nation", "children": [{"id": "44adb0c9-fdcf-45c9-9ab5-dd3a3c5aecd8", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnogenesis, boundary making, racialization, caste, tribe, and indigeneity"}, {"id": "8f529cc5-d718-4a3d-a40d-c9525dbefecf", "type": "concept", "title": "Nationalism, minority status, citizenship, documentation, and belonging"}, {"id": "7678ed53-590c-4af8-8d25-ee7e0e63fd8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity performance, recognition, stigma, and strategic essentialism"}], "description": "Students learn to treat identity categories as historically produced and politically consequential."}, {"id": "bf5002b7-3414-4731-b89c-db2d90cc8143", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Representation and voice", "children": [{"id": "5da83b8d-dc13-4e32-841e-2bbdfa5b3ced", "type": "concept", "title": "Quotation, paraphrase, anonymity, pseudonyms, and narrative authority"}, {"id": "9bb3a42a-2e7a-4903-a0c8-622929f26c6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation ethics, collaborative review, and contested interpretations"}, {"id": "e78a7e7a-453a-4c6b-899a-4cb64f941de4", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding flattening, exoticization, and tokenism"}], "description": "This subtopic asks who gets to speak for a place and how ethnographic writing can include multiple perspectives responsibly."}]}, {"id": "2135759e-160b-4a99-b70f-227ceee8c94f", "type": "topic", "title": "Kinship, Household, Gender, and Personhood", "description": "Students examine how people organize relatedness, care, gender, sexuality, generation, and personhood in regionally specific ways.", "children": [{"id": "c00d75d4-134f-442f-a5b1-e00c9e9a89a4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Kinship and household organization", "children": [{"id": "6a56782c-6e02-4e7d-9881-5089ca98f33c", "type": "concept", "title": "Descent, marriage, adoption, fictive kin, clans, lineages, and chosen kin"}, {"id": "0057ae39-8027-4bcb-a89c-a91463028cea", "type": "concept", "title": "Household composition, inheritance, residence, caregiving, and domestic labor"}, {"id": "9eef4f08-ba7b-408b-9e9b-ae773d58188c", "type": "concept", "title": "Migration, remittances, transnational families, and changing household forms"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how relatedness structures everyday economies, obligation, inheritance, care, and belonging."}, {"id": "5e1cc6b4-92a3-4b23-86c0-0257229ac9d7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender, sexuality, and life course", "children": [{"id": "f23e8a91-404a-458b-a967-40aeb523f584", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender roles, embodiment, masculinity, femininity, and nonbinary categories"}, {"id": "6a9b11d4-7bc4-45a8-b5ae-34e9fa641f6f", "type": "concept", "title": "Sexuality, reproduction, marriage, intimacy, and moral regulation"}, {"id": "7a3f81f2-5a73-436f-9373-9e8891bf13ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Childhood, youth, adulthood, elderhood, and intergenerational authority"}], "description": "Students study gender and sexuality as lived systems of labor, power, desire, morality, and recognition."}, {"id": "7758230f-b88a-4b8a-95d9-a11756bfd354", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Personhood and moral worlds", "children": [{"id": "d445fb44-cc12-4201-92ad-c21022632e4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Dividual, relational, individual, spiritual, and legal personhood"}, {"id": "bdc19f17-7714-4d84-83dd-01f733161f76", "type": "concept", "title": "Honor, shame, respect, care, autonomy, obligation, and moral economy"}, {"id": "b5260ef6-5758-41e6-bb85-14be62554f25", "type": "concept", "title": "Rites of passage, social recognition, and person-making practices"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces local theories of self, obligation, agency, emotion, and social responsibility."}]}, {"id": "5f59c2a7-5b9a-4414-abed-96ffa827a8cc", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Ritual, Media, Art, and Popular Culture", "description": "Students interpret symbolic life through ritual practice, religious movements, media circulation, performance, art, and popular culture.", "children": [{"id": "413a185f-b97c-48e4-a02a-c739e4fb4899", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion and ritual life", "children": [{"id": "71bab1f4-e6f5-4fef-b4c5-8d9a1871f502", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual, pilgrimage, healing, possession, sacrifice, prayer, festivals, and sacred space"}, {"id": "9af62d0f-785b-465f-a725-a194c7820b27", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious authority, reform, conversion, syncretism, and secularism"}, {"id": "fc832a7a-51a4-4f52-8456-8c9fdfbce3b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion, nationalism, gender, conflict, migration, and public life"}], "description": "This subtopic treats religion as embodied practice, institution, narrative, ethics, and power rather than only belief."}, {"id": "c0e8fe77-8b12-4ea2-a6bb-b50346a605de", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Art, performance, and material culture", "children": [{"id": "b38a5319-e32c-4ed0-9226-9d6ac5cb2639", "type": "concept", "title": "Music, dance, craft, textiles, architecture, food, dress, and visual culture"}, {"id": "e1d8f3dd-c679-46fb-8707-1cec1dc20e68", "type": "concept", "title": "Performance, authenticity, heritage, markets, tourism, and appropriation"}, {"id": "013be8f7-5e80-4d4d-8b4b-5b4e70ee03a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums, repatriation, collections, and cultural property debates"}], "description": "Students learn how aesthetic forms carry memory, identity, value, politics, and local theories of skill."}, {"id": "1d042f96-681f-414c-9de6-b1a4606000fd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media and popular culture", "children": [{"id": "5d02d6bf-f881-4633-ab82-749e9836f2f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Publics, counterpublics, censorship, propaganda, and digital activism"}, {"id": "c3d8fad8-8a73-4c13-b981-8c51ef3178bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Popular music, film, serials, comedy, influencers, and fan communities"}, {"id": "fe8fdfb9-5ef7-4152-8654-023e884dc41d", "type": "concept", "title": "Media ethnography, circulation, reception, and platform politics"}], "description": "This subtopic studies how people imagine regions through television, film, radio, social media, memes, and celebrity."}]}, {"id": "2b7ba8c5-c8a5-4ad8-83c5-82ddbc40c3f6", "type": "topic", "title": "Political Economy, Development, Labor, and Inequality", "description": "Students connect local livelihoods and inequality to development, capitalism, state policy, extraction, and global markets.", "children": [{"id": "745a3d52-c9ea-46f3-b018-da0c7f788e16", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Livelihoods and labor", "children": [{"id": "ccb7048d-3d40-4d70-9a10-3370404128bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Agrarian labor, wage work, informal economies, care work, and entrepreneurship"}, {"id": "cd947bd8-f64e-4950-b815-13222e746b6c", "type": "concept", "title": "Precarity, debt, remittances, subcontracting, and platform work"}, {"id": "68ade3dc-f204-4894-afb7-0a8cb0c5c681", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor organizing, exploitation, social protection, and everyday resistance"}], "description": "This subtopic studies how people make a living and how work is shaped by class, gender, migration, and global connection."}, {"id": "cd000755-4768-498f-b849-c7b0a83e85cf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Development and expertise", "children": [{"id": "c4703517-7f58-4dc0-a363-b339cf3aef6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Aid, NGOs, microfinance, humanitarianism, infrastructure, and social programs"}, {"id": "e0236e84-3e15-4f3c-b0ec-5501071683a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Participation, audit culture, indicators, impact evaluation, and project failure"}, {"id": "d17cbe9e-39d6-416b-b9d4-f575daf52c2c", "type": "concept", "title": "Local critique, aspiration, dependency, and development alternatives"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze development as a field of institutions, money, metrics, aspiration, and unintended consequences."}, {"id": "3de8c213-3c7c-4fcb-876a-313e2985d6c6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Inequality and class formation", "children": [{"id": "39e1f733-089f-4d81-a55a-ecdbbfdd7c66", "type": "concept", "title": "Class, caste, race, ethnicity, patronage, clientelism, and elite formation"}, {"id": "c23c6131-e092-4567-8316-504902d9e196", "type": "concept", "title": "Housing, education, health, land, food, water, and infrastructure inequality"}, {"id": "12202010-fac8-4dec-a3d5-cf4792d50235", "type": "concept", "title": "Moral economy, redistribution, corruption, and claims-making"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how wealth, status, land, education, and access are organized in specific regional contexts."}]}, {"id": "b80c859b-920b-4b3b-97ec-42b2e41254f2", "type": "topic", "title": "Urbanization, Migration, Diaspora, and Mobility", "description": "Students study movement and settlement as central to regional life, from rural-urban change to refugee displacement and transnational diaspora.", "children": [{"id": "9a32e23f-a9c9-43fa-aaa6-7f760f83548f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Urbanization and regional cities", "children": [{"id": "6962f1c0-f3ec-41f8-aea3-ac509ba3280f", "type": "concept", "title": "Urban migration, housing, informal settlements, transport, and services"}, {"id": "ff4b1e22-b0a3-468a-a65a-93ed3a279ce6", "type": "concept", "title": "Public space, policing, street economies, neighborhood identity, and planning"}, {"id": "b55da3d0-3164-42e1-a1b0-eafb84bd8de5", "type": "concept", "title": "Mega-cities, secondary cities, border cities, and regional urban networks"}], "description": "This subtopic explores cities as laboratories of class, infrastructure, informality, governance, and cultural creativity."}, {"id": "a0385c4d-d198-4ade-82c3-cbd55b72fdf8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Migration, displacement, and diaspora", "children": [{"id": "db736ca2-ad6f-41ba-8606-1731a7b3e148", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor migration, refugees, asylum, trafficking, study migration, and return"}, {"id": "4eb852af-4160-49bf-9263-843cb099316f", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora institutions, remittances, transnational politics, and long-distance nationalism"}, {"id": "6bdb396b-b5b2-4a13-9f4b-97b0f7dde794", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentation, borders, detention, deportation, and humanitarian regimes"}], "description": "Students analyze why people move, how mobility is governed, and how belonging changes across borders."}, {"id": "066cda3e-87ef-4d69-8558-02d7d4d6872b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mobility and everyday life", "children": [{"id": "69f18bc4-4ad6-4e91-921b-fa18253730cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Roads, ports, airports, digital networks, pilgrimage, and trade routes"}, {"id": "9b51cbed-666f-4b49-8efb-57fcf32da44d", "type": "concept", "title": "Commuting, seasonal movement, tourism, smuggling, and informal corridors"}, {"id": "c8c41dd4-bfad-4b4f-a8cc-ed15ee231a5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Waiting, aspiration, risk, nostalgia, and mobility imaginaries"}], "description": "This subtopic looks at circulation of people, goods, ideas, images, money, and care across the region."}]}, {"id": "a6600c5b-054f-4c8f-adcc-359423519779", "type": "topic", "title": "Environment, Land Use, Extraction, and Climate", "description": "Students examine how land, water, forests, animals, minerals, and climate are embedded in cultural meaning, power, and survival.", "children": [{"id": "1ecdfd1d-cd40-426a-8dd1-4d1e474d715f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Land, territory, and resource use", "children": [{"id": "7f0891d7-55d2-4f05-bd7a-4868067fec7e", "type": "concept", "title": "Customary tenure, private property, commons, conservation, and protected areas"}, {"id": "4d2fe6af-3de3-4815-9130-617e90337e07", "type": "concept", "title": "Agriculture, pastoralism, fishing, forestry, mining, and urban land use"}, {"id": "3c9ac1bd-a8d6-4a52-a45a-0a47f4de3624", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred landscapes, ancestral land, Indigenous sovereignty, and territorial rights"}], "description": "This subtopic studies how people claim, use, inherit, defend, and imagine land and landscapes."}, {"id": "57d2df15-b39c-4f5d-be08-af920c5a7d3b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Extraction and environmental justice", "children": [{"id": "069de4ab-2054-4308-9cb4-a17865717ed3", "type": "concept", "title": "Mines, dams, oil, gas, plantations, fisheries, and infrastructure corridors"}, {"id": "7a9f885e-2516-46e7-ade5-7352b0e6e287", "type": "concept", "title": "Toxicity, displacement, labor, consultation, compensation, and protest"}, {"id": "0dca69e0-7c59-4d33-a1c7-aa56804d0908", "type": "concept", "title": "Corporate responsibility, state violence, legal activism, and community monitoring"}], "description": "Students analyze conflicts around resources and the unequal distribution of harm and benefit."}, {"id": "f414e876-7747-4276-8e2d-1e23b708005b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Climate change and adaptation", "children": [{"id": "80e6a033-a995-419a-990e-2185ad723bec", "type": "concept", "title": "Drought, floods, heat, sea-level rise, food systems, and disaster response"}, {"id": "bb6ad307-f159-4851-8212-258c11185368", "type": "concept", "title": "Adaptation, mitigation, climate finance, loss and damage, and migration"}, {"id": "ea42e2b3-dcb1-42bb-a489-cd38ea9459f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Environmental knowledge, uncertainty, risk, and future-making"}], "description": "This subtopic connects climate impacts to local knowledge, vulnerability, resilience, and political economy."}]}, {"id": "cedd5518-dd03-4e27-aaec-bd637ad74d37", "type": "topic", "title": "States, Governance, Conflict, and Regional Politics", "description": "Students add regional political context to ethnographic analysis without reducing culture to policy or geopolitics.", "children": [{"id": "6dd75bf5-613e-4330-9540-591edee2926a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State formation and everyday governance", "children": [{"id": "bf17dd06-b02d-40d2-83ef-f18c02a833a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Bureaucracy, citizenship, identification, welfare, taxation, and surveillance"}, {"id": "16c58df8-c3e4-4057-bf44-b2e10af60db7", "type": "concept", "title": "Local government, customary authority, chiefs, parties, courts, and religious authority"}, {"id": "4a1964b9-6ffb-41de-81f4-2ec246699f0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Corruption, patronage, clientelism, legitimacy, and everyday negotiation"}], "description": "This subtopic asks how people encounter the state through documents, police, schools, clinics, courts, borders, and infrastructure."}, {"id": "b8948ba6-155d-41f6-8233-5fab6b2a27d2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conflict, security, and violence", "children": [{"id": "a19530e4-8a95-46f3-8c06-cdf432adca86", "type": "concept", "title": "Militarization, policing, insurgency, civil war, gangs, and paramilitaries"}, {"id": "b9d0dca6-ca89-41ed-9383-157bef55fbd3", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory, trauma, reconciliation, transitional justice, and disappearance"}, {"id": "b5f81c28-b3c6-4af3-8a83-debbab9ff5dd", "type": "concept", "title": "Security discourse, borders, counterterrorism, and humanitarian intervention"}], "description": "Students examine how conflict is lived and narrated at household, community, national, and transnational scales."}, {"id": "321bafd5-ebbb-4007-a5eb-72e3cf3659f0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional institutions and geopolitics", "children": [{"id": "138033a5-5a41-4035-bf72-e21487826220", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional organizations, trade blocs, security alliances, and courts"}, {"id": "abb0b0d4-45e7-4ec1-bcbb-1763f43c5523", "type": "concept", "title": "Great-power involvement, aid, sanctions, military bases, and diplomacy"}, {"id": "8df14aac-1589-4230-93d6-a0ed3186d27f", "type": "concept", "title": "How regional politics shape ethnographic fieldwork and local possibilities"}], "description": "This subtopic adds area-studies literacy about regional organizations and external powers."}]}, {"id": "a9123d17-29ee-436f-a90b-d11b1b60867c", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparative and Multi-Sited Regional Analysis", "description": "Students learn to compare ethnographic cases across places without erasing context, difference, history, or scale.", "children": [{"id": "949a5118-5326-466d-83ae-0e2d7059741c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative method in anthropology", "children": [{"id": "3ab8fd72-a857-4809-b6ac-a6fbddb1c636", "type": "concept", "title": "Most-similar and most-different comparisons, process comparison, and controlled comparison"}, {"id": "b4d3abfa-f799-44c2-a853-a563f4f5a257", "type": "concept", "title": "Commensurability, translation, scale, selection bias, and negative cases"}, {"id": "1da8bf49-8806-4f8a-8a52-e0599a261922", "type": "concept", "title": "Using comparison to refine concepts and identify causal or interpretive patterns"}], "description": "This subtopic teaches comparison as an interpretive tool, not a search for simplistic cultural rankings."}, {"id": "f291cea6-38e4-488c-91f7-8d9f317d21ae", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Multi-sited and transregional ethnography", "children": [{"id": "e8e63f4b-dc00-4ce7-bc22-9a6644665243", "type": "concept", "title": "Following people, things, metaphors, stories, conflicts, and policies"}, {"id": "de498e23-70bc-434c-a370-348befa88827", "type": "concept", "title": "Commodity chains, migration circuits, diaspora networks, and expert communities"}, {"id": "5fc880bf-55ec-4d94-8df8-1351097fed0c", "type": "concept", "title": "Fieldwork design, access, depth tradeoffs, and data integration"}], "description": "Students analyze people, objects, money, media, and institutions as they move across places."}, {"id": "3190163b-1740-4892-8188-39be3af5ca6a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional synthesis", "children": [{"id": "abcb120d-017e-4c03-8b78-483807e8dcf5", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparing local cases with regional histories, institutions, and debates"}, {"id": "111668d3-ec98-4656-8522-14c8191a2178", "type": "concept", "title": "Building concept maps, matrices, timelines, and thematic bibliographies"}, {"id": "eb25b837-2652-4142-be0f-50a57731ec57", "type": "concept", "title": "Writing claims that are specific, bounded, and evidence-rich"}], "description": "This subtopic helps students move from case detail to a larger regional argument."}]}, {"id": "3f626d06-07d6-4d06-9374-1a6269abcad1", "type": "topic", "title": "Ethics, Collaboration, Data Sovereignty, and Representation", "description": "Students practice area research as an accountable relationship involving consent, care, collaboration, data protection, and responsible writing.", "children": [{"id": "1bf247fe-1059-4f76-9c46-2c7b83163d82", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Consent, risk, and field relationships", "children": [{"id": "587ce590-b1a3-4c8a-bf5e-db6667df6bb9", "type": "concept", "title": "Informed consent, ongoing consent, confidentiality, anonymity, and withdrawal"}, {"id": "1f77950f-baec-46e4-a635-acb59ecf6c61", "type": "concept", "title": "Power, reciprocity, compensation, gifts, expectations, and community obligations"}, {"id": "3bcc9f83-3a5b-42cb-a53c-c06a846cd4e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk assessment for vulnerable groups, conflict settings, and digital traces"}], "description": "This subtopic teaches students to think ethically before, during, and after research interactions."}, {"id": "285091de-0d99-4708-a111-df9bafa9243b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Collaboration and data sovereignty", "children": [{"id": "fb8ceacb-c756-4476-a94b-6d10f2acefdc", "type": "concept", "title": "Collaborative design, community review, coauthorship, return of findings, and benefit sharing"}, {"id": "b574cff9-10c4-4d2e-8524-1c757ca720a8", "type": "concept", "title": "CARE principles, Indigenous data governance, local archives, and restricted data"}, {"id": "7f4e8eb7-1640-4423-a062-1ea6ea3161fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Archiving, metadata, citation, access controls, and responsible reuse"}], "description": "Students learn why communities may need authority over research questions, records, interpretation, and circulation."}, {"id": "4ab9175d-0827-48f3-a623-5677791967fa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethnographic writing and representation", "children": [{"id": "00c16830-6a27-417a-b8d3-5b335e07bdd3", "type": "concept", "title": "Thick description, scenes, voice, dialogue, composite cases, and pseudonyms"}, {"id": "36783841-0656-45a1-a89b-c70a2b8b76c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Representation crises, Writing Culture debates, and experimental ethnography"}, {"id": "679b5876-7e65-4be5-968f-74ef6a8c39c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Public anthropology, policy briefs, podcasts, exhibits, and multimodal outputs"}], "description": "This subtopic asks how to write vividly and analytically while avoiding harm, exoticization, and overclaiming."}]}, {"id": "c2b7e43e-7d40-4f1b-81b7-45dd5df6620d", "type": "topic", "title": "Regional Research Capstone: Dossier, Annotated Bibliography, and Comparative Paper", "description": "Students synthesize regional history, ethnographic theory, methods, ethics, and comparative analysis into a polished research portfolio.", "children": [{"id": "4a54197b-0bb7-4249-8528-f92ed65f1259", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional research dossier", "children": [{"id": "c265f6cb-56b2-4f4e-a46d-e710c568ff32", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional map, timeline, key terms, actors, institutions, and fieldsite possibilities"}, {"id": "6c5eb537-6220-49b7-9ce3-3b0f0682e19a", "type": "concept", "title": "Language plan, source inventory, ethical risks, and field access considerations"}, {"id": "0eb3f2e1-b082-473d-a9f1-be3cd3d40005", "type": "concept", "title": "Current debates, contemporary issues, and knowledge gaps"}], "description": "The dossier organizes the background knowledge required to make a careful ethnographic argument about a chosen region."}, {"id": "a10f76b9-9f51-4964-adbb-45450066c2db", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Annotated bibliography and evidence base", "children": [{"id": "78de4d1f-d25a-4d06-ac7c-60c54b36e3cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Classic ethnographies, recent peer-reviewed articles, regional histories, and primary sources"}, {"id": "4da58a11-85dc-494b-bcb6-ea28f35c2797", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation quality, source diversity, positionality of authors, and local scholarship"}, {"id": "134c28d6-ed13-43ed-b540-54623726697c", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotations that summarize argument, evidence, method, and relevance"}], "description": "Students learn to build a balanced source base across ethnography, history, theory, media, and policy."}, {"id": "bc66aba0-382c-4f21-a9a7-f011a362270e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative regional research paper", "children": [{"id": "5a24d0dc-0fb0-4aba-99ec-b29248c8c829", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question, thesis, literature review, case evidence, and comparative frame"}, {"id": "f3153f23-e9f0-4192-bd0d-f0e373adf93c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnographic vignettes, historical context, maps, tables, and analytic concepts"}, {"id": "eb6e3434-c0a8-4eba-9d53-99c83d308c43", "type": "concept", "title": "Limitations, ethics statement, public-facing abstract, and revision process"}], "description": "The capstone asks students to make a bounded, evidence-driven argument while reflecting on method and representation."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$ethajson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'ethnographic-area-studies'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Ethnographic Area Studies',
  goal = 'Ethnographic Area Studies',
  summary = 'Students ask what a region is, who defines it, and how ethnography can study place without freezing cultures into bounded containers. They build historical depth for contemporary cases, design responsible regionally informed research, and analyze colonialism, language and identity, kinship and personhood, symbolic life, political economy, mobility, land and climate, and governance without reducing culture to policy. The course adds comparative and multi-sited analysis, ethics and representation, and a regional research capstone.',
  data = $ethajson${"slug": "ethnographic-area-studies", "title": "Ethnographic Area Studies", "description": "Students ask what a region is, who defines it, and how ethnography can study place without freezing cultures into bounded containers. They build historical depth for contemporary cases, design responsible regionally informed research, and analyze colonialism, language and identity, kinship and personhood, symbolic life, political economy, mobility, land and climate, and governance without reducing culture to policy. The course adds comparative and multi-sited analysis, ethics and representation, and a regional research capstone.", "topics": [{"id": "6751200e-7f2f-46ea-b476-570277970ca6", "type": "topic", "title": "Area Studies, Anthropology, and the Problem of Region", "description": "Students begin by asking what a region is, who defines it, and how ethnography can study place without freezing cultures into bounded containers.", "children": [{"id": "02bc25ed-d4d0-42ce-890c-569ff2b54209", "type": "subtopic", "title": "What counts as a region", "children": [{"id": "b42d3c46-1c46-42e0-af9f-e4d10ed6ef5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Culture areas, nation-states, borderlands, oceans, diasporas, and transregional circuits"}, {"id": "5e528ef7-5ea7-41c2-aadb-042f4b142b8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Scale: village, city, province, nation, region, empire, and globe"}, {"id": "0edf9f5e-64b4-4058-859e-0d22e555b450", "type": "concept", "title": "Risks of essentialism, exceptionalism, and area stereotypes"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces region as an analytic, historical, political, and lived category rather than a neutral map label."}, {"id": "e3c0fcdc-b632-4da3-9d9c-69b15bb2845c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Anthropology and area studies", "children": [{"id": "ccad6cfc-84f1-40ad-9fcb-39b04e040ed9", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnography, participant observation, and local categories of meaning"}, {"id": "943d368f-7bc2-4b72-9d41-c110eefae4f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparison with political science, history, sociology, geography, and international relations"}, {"id": "af6872e2-34b5-4769-a511-8bb515eb3bec", "type": "concept", "title": "Area expertise, language study, archives, media, policy, and field access"}], "description": "Students learn how anthropology contributes grounded, everyday, language-sensitive knowledge to broader regional scholarship."}, {"id": "a9084f21-79c2-49f9-819e-92ea67acb1bd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reflexivity and positionality", "children": [{"id": "5347ba9b-fccf-44d8-8f08-a20befa5589a", "type": "concept", "title": "Researcher identity, insider/outsider status, and accountability"}, {"id": "e41b9b54-bb47-4641-b1d3-8610522fd5c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Orientalism, colonial knowledge, extraction, and representation"}, {"id": "4049724c-7cdc-47f4-9a6b-a31a2cc5eaec", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethical humility, uncertainty, and responsible claims about place"}], "description": "This subtopic frames area research as a relationship shaped by biography, power, language, mobility, and institutional location."}]}, {"id": "30f94f8c-579b-420e-b6b6-407133eadfd9", "type": "topic", "title": "Regional Histories and Anthropological Debates", "description": "Students build enough historical depth to understand contemporary ethnographic cases as products of long-term social, political, and cultural change.", "children": [{"id": "20c0df8c-44ee-48ed-b5b0-dfe8cdd860ab", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical foundations", "children": [{"id": "b73bd130-e28c-4536-ba5f-1a7d969ac766", "type": "concept", "title": "Precolonial formations, state systems, trade networks, and religious movements"}, {"id": "a95caf4f-148a-4e87-ad08-027fd63b3691", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial rule, border making, labor regimes, and missionization"}, {"id": "dad0d38c-f5fa-4714-aecf-1044800424c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Independence, revolution, development projects, and contemporary transformations"}], "description": "Area studies requires students to see the present as historically produced, not as timeless culture."}, {"id": "ee0e0e37-390b-416b-8808-868e67a3eefd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Key anthropological debates by region", "children": [{"id": "34a69251-a95f-41eb-9a4c-69d31e4b523b", "type": "concept", "title": "Classic ethnographies, revisionist scholarship, and contemporary field debates"}, {"id": "70e80124-ab34-4033-919c-67bf3e0f4edf", "type": "concept", "title": "Local concepts of personhood, authority, morality, economy, and belonging"}, {"id": "fc8c7ebf-412a-4a31-8aee-f22b3c8947b2", "type": "concept", "title": "Debates over tradition, modernity, nationalism, globalization, and indigeneity"}], "description": "Students learn to identify the arguments that have shaped scholarship in the region they choose."}, {"id": "1b5837e0-3f98-4fa4-9643-646e8e5a8c4e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading across disciplines", "children": [{"id": "1a946926-77a9-467b-bec9-6581290bbb37", "type": "concept", "title": "Scholarly monographs, articles, archives, oral histories, and primary sources"}, {"id": "cde11604-4afd-449a-ae74-d628410015a9", "type": "concept", "title": "News, government documents, NGO reports, cultural production, and social media"}, {"id": "1dc8840a-e9f0-40aa-bc7b-8f5023686ed6", "type": "concept", "title": "Evaluating disciplinary assumptions and evidentiary standards"}], "description": "This subtopic teaches students to combine ethnography with history, political economy, media, and policy sources."}]}, {"id": "5f8cb2bb-d95e-40d8-a21f-3ba8f9af9af5", "type": "topic", "title": "Ethnographic Methods for Area Research", "description": "Students learn how to design responsible, regionally informed research using observation, interviews, fieldnotes, archives, and comparative evidence.", "children": [{"id": "995c0412-26e7-407d-89a9-4a9b7087a40a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fieldsite selection and research design", "children": [{"id": "20a9d5ba-6a90-4ccf-a976-7c2b1b8ea98b", "type": "concept", "title": "Research questions, case selection, access, feasibility, and language needs"}, {"id": "5cba0032-49a1-412b-930e-3aa701dcab7e", "type": "concept", "title": "Single-sited, multi-sited, virtual, institutional, and archival fieldwork"}, {"id": "41470754-f131-4cc9-a598-922f3e68a195", "type": "concept", "title": "Sampling, interlocutor diversity, triangulation, and scope control"}], "description": "This subtopic turns regional curiosity into a workable research question and evidence plan."}, {"id": "758cf3de-003e-410e-9cdc-04751f011dd6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Participant observation and interviewing", "children": [{"id": "43f14567-4516-4ef8-9789-8554c2cadc20", "type": "concept", "title": "Observation, participation, rapport, field roles, and everyday routines"}, {"id": "16a1b838-9b38-4227-9986-24c689844d5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Semi-structured interviews, oral histories, life histories, and focus groups"}, {"id": "eea60f59-3161-46e2-be60-a8c4705c71ca", "type": "concept", "title": "Recording, transcription, translation, confidentiality, and follow-up"}], "description": "Students practice the core methods that make ethnographic area studies attentive to lived experience."}, {"id": "c74e4abe-62cd-492f-9dae-d73723026dec", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fieldnotes, coding, and analysis", "children": [{"id": "52629c54-516a-41cf-a6b7-accf9f55effe", "type": "concept", "title": "Jottings, expanded fieldnotes, analytic memos, and reflexive notes"}, {"id": "5e2642da-9a44-42b2-96be-a2367ab7eb1d", "type": "concept", "title": "Coding, thematic analysis, narrative analysis, and negative cases"}, {"id": "6f581035-2d08-43fc-a959-39ceaf288f4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Building claims from evidence while preserving ambiguity and context"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how raw experience becomes organized evidence through disciplined writing and interpretation."}]}, {"id": "41c9782d-2554-4379-9ddf-1ed272a0c36d", "type": "topic", "title": "Colonialism, Postcoloniality, and Global Power", "description": "Students analyze how colonial rule, empire, capitalism, and global hierarchy continue to shape regional identities, institutions, and inequalities.", "children": [{"id": "ec6a0186-2d18-47ed-9cc3-1575c2fb0f2f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Colonial knowledge and governance", "children": [{"id": "dd2bda25-dce1-4354-ab52-e04bb0ed2095", "type": "concept", "title": "Census, maps, law, ethnology, museums, missions, schools, and archives"}, {"id": "3c719fa8-4512-42e0-929a-9699891dd381", "type": "concept", "title": "Indirect rule, settler colonialism, plantations, extraction, and labor migration"}, {"id": "ea0499b4-6f32-4cda-b3e5-f33543086886", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, tribe, caste, ethnicity, religion, and administrative categories"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how colonial institutions categorized people, land, labor, religion, and culture."}, {"id": "de609975-326b-4773-9988-a9ed959429e4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postcolonial states and memory", "children": [{"id": "c2ca6609-7bbb-4d11-88c9-24ebff5a5ffc", "type": "concept", "title": "Nation building, citizenship, borders, minorities, and official histories"}, {"id": "e9308f13-a2fc-45f4-9e37-9bbde33bfe27", "type": "concept", "title": "Resistance, decolonization, liberation movements, and civil conflict"}, {"id": "80a6aaea-2846-49d5-949e-5f935f1c8106", "type": "concept", "title": "Heritage, monuments, museums, reparations, and contested memory"}], "description": "Students study how independence, nationalism, violence, and memory politics shape present-day social life."}, {"id": "b9f4f81a-cfe5-45db-a5c6-619ddcfe2192", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global capitalism and unequal connection", "children": [{"id": "359a91be-28cc-4bdc-a02b-b4d21d3ca2e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Dependency, world systems, structural adjustment, debt, and development expertise"}, {"id": "e5b6a46b-27a4-4ec4-8274-a1639a9fda67", "type": "concept", "title": "Commodity frontiers, logistics, tourism, migration, and remittances"}, {"id": "d71773d6-4aea-4cb8-9812-4928a173e229", "type": "concept", "title": "NGOs, international organizations, corporations, and humanitarian power"}], "description": "This subtopic connects local lives to commodity chains, finance, aid, development, and geopolitics."}]}, {"id": "9890ea01-de9d-4b00-b3a0-10f91687cc9f", "type": "topic", "title": "Language, Translation, Ethnicity, and Identity", "description": "Students learn how language, naming, translation, and identity categories organize social life and shape what researchers can understand.", "children": [{"id": "3f46d545-6669-4bf5-8e22-d909aa88ab06", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language and cultural fluency", "children": [{"id": "a7445d4d-b959-4cf5-be43-b0272ee96686", "type": "concept", "title": "Language learning plans, interpreters, translation, transcription, and glossing"}, {"id": "85579318-85a2-4f3b-9d7f-cb8befc4ae82", "type": "concept", "title": "Code-switching, multilingualism, honorifics, speech genres, and pragmatics"}, {"id": "1c7e8313-8bdb-498d-a917-8ad666f0f3e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Untranslatable concepts, emic terms, and analytic translation choices"}], "description": "Area expertise depends on more than vocabulary; it requires attention to registers, genres, humor, silence, and local categories."}, {"id": "4f48a348-d5b6-46c3-8dce-d63dcf3d4b62", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethnicity, race, caste, indigeneity, and nation", "children": [{"id": "44adb0c9-fdcf-45c9-9ab5-dd3a3c5aecd8", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnogenesis, boundary making, racialization, caste, tribe, and indigeneity"}, {"id": "8f529cc5-d718-4a3d-a40d-c9525dbefecf", "type": "concept", "title": "Nationalism, minority status, citizenship, documentation, and belonging"}, {"id": "7678ed53-590c-4af8-8d25-ee7e0e63fd8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity performance, recognition, stigma, and strategic essentialism"}], "description": "Students learn to treat identity categories as historically produced and politically consequential."}, {"id": "bf5002b7-3414-4731-b89c-db2d90cc8143", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Representation and voice", "children": [{"id": "5da83b8d-dc13-4e32-841e-2bbdfa5b3ced", "type": "concept", "title": "Quotation, paraphrase, anonymity, pseudonyms, and narrative authority"}, {"id": "9bb3a42a-2e7a-4903-a0c8-622929f26c6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation ethics, collaborative review, and contested interpretations"}, {"id": "e78a7e7a-453a-4c6b-899a-4cb64f941de4", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding flattening, exoticization, and tokenism"}], "description": "This subtopic asks who gets to speak for a place and how ethnographic writing can include multiple perspectives responsibly."}]}, {"id": "2135759e-160b-4a99-b70f-227ceee8c94f", "type": "topic", "title": "Kinship, Household, Gender, and Personhood", "description": "Students examine how people organize relatedness, care, gender, sexuality, generation, and personhood in regionally specific ways.", "children": [{"id": "c00d75d4-134f-442f-a5b1-e00c9e9a89a4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Kinship and household organization", "children": [{"id": "6a56782c-6e02-4e7d-9881-5089ca98f33c", "type": "concept", "title": "Descent, marriage, adoption, fictive kin, clans, lineages, and chosen kin"}, {"id": "0057ae39-8027-4bcb-a89c-a91463028cea", "type": "concept", "title": "Household composition, inheritance, residence, caregiving, and domestic labor"}, {"id": "9eef4f08-ba7b-408b-9e9b-ae773d58188c", "type": "concept", "title": "Migration, remittances, transnational families, and changing household forms"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how relatedness structures everyday economies, obligation, inheritance, care, and belonging."}, {"id": "5e1cc6b4-92a3-4b23-86c0-0257229ac9d7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender, sexuality, and life course", "children": [{"id": "f23e8a91-404a-458b-a967-40aeb523f584", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender roles, embodiment, masculinity, femininity, and nonbinary categories"}, {"id": "6a9b11d4-7bc4-45a8-b5ae-34e9fa641f6f", "type": "concept", "title": "Sexuality, reproduction, marriage, intimacy, and moral regulation"}, {"id": "7a3f81f2-5a73-436f-9373-9e8891bf13ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Childhood, youth, adulthood, elderhood, and intergenerational authority"}], "description": "Students study gender and sexuality as lived systems of labor, power, desire, morality, and recognition."}, {"id": "7758230f-b88a-4b8a-95d9-a11756bfd354", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Personhood and moral worlds", "children": [{"id": "d445fb44-cc12-4201-92ad-c21022632e4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Dividual, relational, individual, spiritual, and legal personhood"}, {"id": "bdc19f17-7714-4d84-83dd-01f733161f76", "type": "concept", "title": "Honor, shame, respect, care, autonomy, obligation, and moral economy"}, {"id": "b5260ef6-5758-41e6-bb85-14be62554f25", "type": "concept", "title": "Rites of passage, social recognition, and person-making practices"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces local theories of self, obligation, agency, emotion, and social responsibility."}]}, {"id": "5f59c2a7-5b9a-4414-abed-96ffa827a8cc", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Ritual, Media, Art, and Popular Culture", "description": "Students interpret symbolic life through ritual practice, religious movements, media circulation, performance, art, and popular culture.", "children": [{"id": "413a185f-b97c-48e4-a02a-c739e4fb4899", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion and ritual life", "children": [{"id": "71bab1f4-e6f5-4fef-b4c5-8d9a1871f502", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual, pilgrimage, healing, possession, sacrifice, prayer, festivals, and sacred space"}, {"id": "9af62d0f-785b-465f-a725-a194c7820b27", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious authority, reform, conversion, syncretism, and secularism"}, {"id": "fc832a7a-51a4-4f52-8456-8c9fdfbce3b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion, nationalism, gender, conflict, migration, and public life"}], "description": "This subtopic treats religion as embodied practice, institution, narrative, ethics, and power rather than only belief."}, {"id": "c0e8fe77-8b12-4ea2-a6bb-b50346a605de", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Art, performance, and material culture", "children": [{"id": "b38a5319-e32c-4ed0-9226-9d6ac5cb2639", "type": "concept", "title": "Music, dance, craft, textiles, architecture, food, dress, and visual culture"}, {"id": "e1d8f3dd-c679-46fb-8707-1cec1dc20e68", "type": "concept", "title": "Performance, authenticity, heritage, markets, tourism, and appropriation"}, {"id": "013be8f7-5e80-4d4d-8b4b-5b4e70ee03a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums, repatriation, collections, and cultural property debates"}], "description": "Students learn how aesthetic forms carry memory, identity, value, politics, and local theories of skill."}, {"id": "1d042f96-681f-414c-9de6-b1a4606000fd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media and popular culture", "children": [{"id": "5d02d6bf-f881-4633-ab82-749e9836f2f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Publics, counterpublics, censorship, propaganda, and digital activism"}, {"id": "c3d8fad8-8a73-4c13-b981-8c51ef3178bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Popular music, film, serials, comedy, influencers, and fan communities"}, {"id": "fe8fdfb9-5ef7-4152-8654-023e884dc41d", "type": "concept", "title": "Media ethnography, circulation, reception, and platform politics"}], "description": "This subtopic studies how people imagine regions through television, film, radio, social media, memes, and celebrity."}]}, {"id": "2b7ba8c5-c8a5-4ad8-83c5-82ddbc40c3f6", "type": "topic", "title": "Political Economy, Development, Labor, and Inequality", "description": "Students connect local livelihoods and inequality to development, capitalism, state policy, extraction, and global markets.", "children": [{"id": "745a3d52-c9ea-46f3-b018-da0c7f788e16", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Livelihoods and labor", "children": [{"id": "ccb7048d-3d40-4d70-9a10-3370404128bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Agrarian labor, wage work, informal economies, care work, and entrepreneurship"}, {"id": "cd947bd8-f64e-4950-b815-13222e746b6c", "type": "concept", "title": "Precarity, debt, remittances, subcontracting, and platform work"}, {"id": "68ade3dc-f204-4894-afb7-0a8cb0c5c681", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor organizing, exploitation, social protection, and everyday resistance"}], "description": "This subtopic studies how people make a living and how work is shaped by class, gender, migration, and global connection."}, {"id": "cd000755-4768-498f-b849-c7b0a83e85cf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Development and expertise", "children": [{"id": "c4703517-7f58-4dc0-a363-b339cf3aef6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Aid, NGOs, microfinance, humanitarianism, infrastructure, and social programs"}, {"id": "e0236e84-3e15-4f3c-b0ec-5501071683a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Participation, audit culture, indicators, impact evaluation, and project failure"}, {"id": "d17cbe9e-39d6-416b-b9d4-f575daf52c2c", "type": "concept", "title": "Local critique, aspiration, dependency, and development alternatives"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze development as a field of institutions, money, metrics, aspiration, and unintended consequences."}, {"id": "3de8c213-3c7c-4fcb-876a-313e2985d6c6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Inequality and class formation", "children": [{"id": "39e1f733-089f-4d81-a55a-ecdbbfdd7c66", "type": "concept", "title": "Class, caste, race, ethnicity, patronage, clientelism, and elite formation"}, {"id": "c23c6131-e092-4567-8316-504902d9e196", "type": "concept", "title": "Housing, education, health, land, food, water, and infrastructure inequality"}, {"id": "12202010-fac8-4dec-a3d5-cf4792d50235", "type": "concept", "title": "Moral economy, redistribution, corruption, and claims-making"}], "description": "This subtopic examines how wealth, status, land, education, and access are organized in specific regional contexts."}]}, {"id": "b80c859b-920b-4b3b-97ec-42b2e41254f2", "type": "topic", "title": "Urbanization, Migration, Diaspora, and Mobility", "description": "Students study movement and settlement as central to regional life, from rural-urban change to refugee displacement and transnational diaspora.", "children": [{"id": "9a32e23f-a9c9-43fa-aaa6-7f760f83548f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Urbanization and regional cities", "children": [{"id": "6962f1c0-f3ec-41f8-aea3-ac509ba3280f", "type": "concept", "title": "Urban migration, housing, informal settlements, transport, and services"}, {"id": "ff4b1e22-b0a3-468a-a65a-93ed3a279ce6", "type": "concept", "title": "Public space, policing, street economies, neighborhood identity, and planning"}, {"id": "b55da3d0-3164-42e1-a1b0-eafb84bd8de5", "type": "concept", "title": "Mega-cities, secondary cities, border cities, and regional urban networks"}], "description": "This subtopic explores cities as laboratories of class, infrastructure, informality, governance, and cultural creativity."}, {"id": "a0385c4d-d198-4ade-82c3-cbd55b72fdf8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Migration, displacement, and diaspora", "children": [{"id": "db736ca2-ad6f-41ba-8606-1731a7b3e148", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor migration, refugees, asylum, trafficking, study migration, and return"}, {"id": "4eb852af-4160-49bf-9263-843cb099316f", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora institutions, remittances, transnational politics, and long-distance nationalism"}, {"id": "6bdb396b-b5b2-4a13-9f4b-97b0f7dde794", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentation, borders, detention, deportation, and humanitarian regimes"}], "description": "Students analyze why people move, how mobility is governed, and how belonging changes across borders."}, {"id": "066cda3e-87ef-4d69-8558-02d7d4d6872b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mobility and everyday life", "children": [{"id": "69f18bc4-4ad6-4e91-921b-fa18253730cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Roads, ports, airports, digital networks, pilgrimage, and trade routes"}, {"id": "9b51cbed-666f-4b49-8efb-57fcf32da44d", "type": "concept", "title": "Commuting, seasonal movement, tourism, smuggling, and informal corridors"}, {"id": "c8c41dd4-bfad-4b4f-a8cc-ed15ee231a5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Waiting, aspiration, risk, nostalgia, and mobility imaginaries"}], "description": "This subtopic looks at circulation of people, goods, ideas, images, money, and care across the region."}]}, {"id": "a6600c5b-054f-4c8f-adcc-359423519779", "type": "topic", "title": "Environment, Land Use, Extraction, and Climate", "description": "Students examine how land, water, forests, animals, minerals, and climate are embedded in cultural meaning, power, and survival.", "children": [{"id": "1ecdfd1d-cd40-426a-8dd1-4d1e474d715f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Land, territory, and resource use", "children": [{"id": "7f0891d7-55d2-4f05-bd7a-4868067fec7e", "type": "concept", "title": "Customary tenure, private property, commons, conservation, and protected areas"}, {"id": "4d2fe6af-3de3-4815-9130-617e90337e07", "type": "concept", "title": "Agriculture, pastoralism, fishing, forestry, mining, and urban land use"}, {"id": "3c9ac1bd-a8d6-4a52-a45a-0a47f4de3624", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred landscapes, ancestral land, Indigenous sovereignty, and territorial rights"}], "description": "This subtopic studies how people claim, use, inherit, defend, and imagine land and landscapes."}, {"id": "57d2df15-b39c-4f5d-be08-af920c5a7d3b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Extraction and environmental justice", "children": [{"id": "069de4ab-2054-4308-9cb4-a17865717ed3", "type": "concept", "title": "Mines, dams, oil, gas, plantations, fisheries, and infrastructure corridors"}, {"id": "7a9f885e-2516-46e7-ade5-7352b0e6e287", "type": "concept", "title": "Toxicity, displacement, labor, consultation, compensation, and protest"}, {"id": "0dca69e0-7c59-4d33-a1c7-aa56804d0908", "type": "concept", "title": "Corporate responsibility, state violence, legal activism, and community monitoring"}], "description": "Students analyze conflicts around resources and the unequal distribution of harm and benefit."}, {"id": "f414e876-7747-4276-8e2d-1e23b708005b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Climate change and adaptation", "children": [{"id": "80e6a033-a995-419a-990e-2185ad723bec", "type": "concept", "title": "Drought, floods, heat, sea-level rise, food systems, and disaster response"}, {"id": "bb6ad307-f159-4851-8212-258c11185368", "type": "concept", "title": "Adaptation, mitigation, climate finance, loss and damage, and migration"}, {"id": "ea42e2b3-dcb1-42bb-a489-cd38ea9459f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Environmental knowledge, uncertainty, risk, and future-making"}], "description": "This subtopic connects climate impacts to local knowledge, vulnerability, resilience, and political economy."}]}, {"id": "cedd5518-dd03-4e27-aaec-bd637ad74d37", "type": "topic", "title": "States, Governance, Conflict, and Regional Politics", "description": "Students add regional political context to ethnographic analysis without reducing culture to policy or geopolitics.", "children": [{"id": "6dd75bf5-613e-4330-9540-591edee2926a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State formation and everyday governance", "children": [{"id": "bf17dd06-b02d-40d2-83ef-f18c02a833a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Bureaucracy, citizenship, identification, welfare, taxation, and surveillance"}, {"id": "16c58df8-c3e4-4057-bf44-b2e10af60db7", "type": "concept", "title": "Local government, customary authority, chiefs, parties, courts, and religious authority"}, {"id": "4a1964b9-6ffb-41de-81f4-2ec246699f0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Corruption, patronage, clientelism, legitimacy, and everyday negotiation"}], "description": "This subtopic asks how people encounter the state through documents, police, schools, clinics, courts, borders, and infrastructure."}, {"id": "b8948ba6-155d-41f6-8233-5fab6b2a27d2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conflict, security, and violence", "children": [{"id": "a19530e4-8a95-46f3-8c06-cdf432adca86", "type": "concept", "title": "Militarization, policing, insurgency, civil war, gangs, and paramilitaries"}, {"id": "b9d0dca6-ca89-41ed-9383-157bef55fbd3", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory, trauma, reconciliation, transitional justice, and disappearance"}, {"id": "b5f81c28-b3c6-4af3-8a83-debbab9ff5dd", "type": "concept", "title": "Security discourse, borders, counterterrorism, and humanitarian intervention"}], "description": "Students examine how conflict is lived and narrated at household, community, national, and transnational scales."}, {"id": "321bafd5-ebbb-4007-a5eb-72e3cf3659f0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional institutions and geopolitics", "children": [{"id": "138033a5-5a41-4035-bf72-e21487826220", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional organizations, trade blocs, security alliances, and courts"}, {"id": "abb0b0d4-45e7-4ec1-bcbb-1763f43c5523", "type": "concept", "title": "Great-power involvement, aid, sanctions, military bases, and diplomacy"}, {"id": "8df14aac-1589-4230-93d6-a0ed3186d27f", "type": "concept", "title": "How regional politics shape ethnographic fieldwork and local possibilities"}], "description": "This subtopic adds area-studies literacy about regional organizations and external powers."}]}, {"id": "a9123d17-29ee-436f-a90b-d11b1b60867c", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparative and Multi-Sited Regional Analysis", "description": "Students learn to compare ethnographic cases across places without erasing context, difference, history, or scale.", "children": [{"id": "949a5118-5326-466d-83ae-0e2d7059741c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative method in anthropology", "children": [{"id": "3ab8fd72-a857-4809-b6ac-a6fbddb1c636", "type": "concept", "title": "Most-similar and most-different comparisons, process comparison, and controlled comparison"}, {"id": "b4d3abfa-f799-44c2-a853-a563f4f5a257", "type": "concept", "title": "Commensurability, translation, scale, selection bias, and negative cases"}, {"id": "1da8bf49-8806-4f8a-8a52-e0599a261922", "type": "concept", "title": "Using comparison to refine concepts and identify causal or interpretive patterns"}], "description": "This subtopic teaches comparison as an interpretive tool, not a search for simplistic cultural rankings."}, {"id": "f291cea6-38e4-488c-91f7-8d9f317d21ae", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Multi-sited and transregional ethnography", "children": [{"id": "e8e63f4b-dc00-4ce7-bc22-9a6644665243", "type": "concept", "title": "Following people, things, metaphors, stories, conflicts, and policies"}, {"id": "de498e23-70bc-434c-a370-348befa88827", "type": "concept", "title": "Commodity chains, migration circuits, diaspora networks, and expert communities"}, {"id": "5fc880bf-55ec-4d94-8df8-1351097fed0c", "type": "concept", "title": "Fieldwork design, access, depth tradeoffs, and data integration"}], "description": "Students analyze people, objects, money, media, and institutions as they move across places."}, {"id": "3190163b-1740-4892-8188-39be3af5ca6a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional synthesis", "children": [{"id": "abcb120d-017e-4c03-8b78-483807e8dcf5", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparing local cases with regional histories, institutions, and debates"}, {"id": "111668d3-ec98-4656-8522-14c8191a2178", "type": "concept", "title": "Building concept maps, matrices, timelines, and thematic bibliographies"}, {"id": "eb25b837-2652-4142-be0f-50a57731ec57", "type": "concept", "title": "Writing claims that are specific, bounded, and evidence-rich"}], "description": "This subtopic helps students move from case detail to a larger regional argument."}]}, {"id": "3f626d06-07d6-4d06-9374-1a6269abcad1", "type": "topic", "title": "Ethics, Collaboration, Data Sovereignty, and Representation", "description": "Students practice area research as an accountable relationship involving consent, care, collaboration, data protection, and responsible writing.", "children": [{"id": "1bf247fe-1059-4f76-9c46-2c7b83163d82", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Consent, risk, and field relationships", "children": [{"id": "587ce590-b1a3-4c8a-bf5e-db6667df6bb9", "type": "concept", "title": "Informed consent, ongoing consent, confidentiality, anonymity, and withdrawal"}, {"id": "1f77950f-baec-46e4-a635-acb59ecf6c61", "type": "concept", "title": "Power, reciprocity, compensation, gifts, expectations, and community obligations"}, {"id": "3bcc9f83-3a5b-42cb-a53c-c06a846cd4e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk assessment for vulnerable groups, conflict settings, and digital traces"}], "description": "This subtopic teaches students to think ethically before, during, and after research interactions."}, {"id": "285091de-0d99-4708-a111-df9bafa9243b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Collaboration and data sovereignty", "children": [{"id": "fb8ceacb-c756-4476-a94b-6d10f2acefdc", "type": "concept", "title": "Collaborative design, community review, coauthorship, return of findings, and benefit sharing"}, {"id": "b574cff9-10c4-4d2e-8524-1c757ca720a8", "type": "concept", "title": "CARE principles, Indigenous data governance, local archives, and restricted data"}, {"id": "7f4e8eb7-1640-4423-a062-1ea6ea3161fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Archiving, metadata, citation, access controls, and responsible reuse"}], "description": "Students learn why communities may need authority over research questions, records, interpretation, and circulation."}, {"id": "4ab9175d-0827-48f3-a623-5677791967fa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethnographic writing and representation", "children": [{"id": "00c16830-6a27-417a-b8d3-5b335e07bdd3", "type": "concept", "title": "Thick description, scenes, voice, dialogue, composite cases, and pseudonyms"}, {"id": "36783841-0656-45a1-a89b-c70a2b8b76c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Representation crises, Writing Culture debates, and experimental ethnography"}, {"id": "679b5876-7e65-4be5-968f-74ef6a8c39c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Public anthropology, policy briefs, podcasts, exhibits, and multimodal outputs"}], "description": "This subtopic asks how to write vividly and analytically while avoiding harm, exoticization, and overclaiming."}]}, {"id": "c2b7e43e-7d40-4f1b-81b7-45dd5df6620d", "type": "topic", "title": "Regional Research Capstone: Dossier, Annotated Bibliography, and Comparative Paper", "description": "Students synthesize regional history, ethnographic theory, methods, ethics, and comparative analysis into a polished research portfolio.", "children": [{"id": "4a54197b-0bb7-4249-8528-f92ed65f1259", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional research dossier", "children": [{"id": "c265f6cb-56b2-4f4e-a46d-e710c568ff32", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional map, timeline, key terms, actors, institutions, and fieldsite possibilities"}, {"id": "6c5eb537-6220-49b7-9ce3-3b0f0682e19a", "type": "concept", "title": "Language plan, source inventory, ethical risks, and field access considerations"}, {"id": "0eb3f2e1-b082-473d-a9f1-be3cd3d40005", "type": "concept", "title": "Current debates, contemporary issues, and knowledge gaps"}], "description": "The dossier organizes the background knowledge required to make a careful ethnographic argument about a chosen region."}, {"id": "a10f76b9-9f51-4964-adbb-45450066c2db", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Annotated bibliography and evidence base", "children": [{"id": "78de4d1f-d25a-4d06-ac7c-60c54b36e3cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Classic ethnographies, recent peer-reviewed articles, regional histories, and primary sources"}, {"id": "4da58a11-85dc-494b-bcb6-ea28f35c2797", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation quality, source diversity, positionality of authors, and local scholarship"}, {"id": "134c28d6-ed13-43ed-b540-54623726697c", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotations that summarize argument, evidence, method, and relevance"}], "description": "Students learn to build a balanced source base across ethnography, history, theory, media, and policy."}, {"id": "bc66aba0-382c-4f21-a9a7-f011a362270e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative regional research paper", "children": [{"id": "5a24d0dc-0fb0-4aba-99ec-b29248c8c829", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question, thesis, literature review, case evidence, and comparative frame"}, {"id": "f3153f23-e9f0-4192-bd0d-f0e373adf93c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnographic vignettes, historical context, maps, tables, and analytic concepts"}, {"id": "eb6e3434-c0a8-4eba-9d53-99c83d308c43", "type": "concept", "title": "Limitations, ethics statement, public-facing abstract, and revision process"}], "description": "The capstone asks students to make a bounded, evidence-driven argument while reflecting on method and representation."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$ethajson$::jsonb,
